  economics:{"figures": [{"v": "Rs3.5 bn", "l": "subsidy ceiling, Sep 2025–Jun 2026", "c": 14}, {"v": "Rs700 bn", "l": "merchant payments a year the subsidy was expected to cover", "c": 15}, {"v": "Rs21 bn", "l": "cost of printing banknotes in FY2023", "c": 13}, {"v": "Rs12.07 trn", "l": "currency in circulation, 27 Mar 2026", "c": 32}, {"v": "US$13.7 m", "l": "Gates Foundation grant to Karandaaz for Raast over 48 months (Dec 2025)", "c": 23}, {"v": "Rs0.5 trn", "l": "QR merchant payments in Jan–Mar 2026 (87.3 m payments)", "c": 6}], "p": ["Who pays: the federal budget, through a technical supplementary grant, pays up to 0.5% of each Raast QR payment (capped at Rs100) to the two institutions involved [14][15]. The Rs3.5 billion ceiling matches 0.5% of the Rs700 billion of merchant payments the government expected to cover (CGTK calculation) [15]. Merchants may still pay up to 0.25%, so acceptance is cheap but not necessarily free [14].", "Who gains: banks and wallets receive income on payments that would otherwise earn nothing, which is why Kayani asked them to do more in return [2]. Merchants gain cheaper acceptance than cards, which cost 1.5–2.5% under the 2020 framework [13]. The state gains if cash handling falls: the subsidy ceiling is about one-sixth of the FY2023 cost of printing banknotes (CGTK calculation) [14][13], and digital records widen the tax base.", "Value for money is not yet shown. Merchant payments grew from 36.3 million to 55.9 million in one quarter [5], but currency in circulation rose 28.4% in the year to March 2026 [32]. Without the SBP’s impact report, the public cannot tell how much of the growth the subsidy caused. Outside funding is modest: the Gates Foundation committed US$13.7 million to Karandaaz in December 2025 for Raast oversight, wider use and links with six Gulf countries [23]."]},
